\description{
A secondary parameter needs a result from a second profile:  renal clearance
divides an amount excreted in urine by a plasma AUC, an accumulation ratio
compares one dosing interval with another, and a metabolite ratio compares
two analytes.  This adds the request and the linkage columns
(\code{interval_id} on the reference interval and \verb{<param>_ref} on the intervals
calculating the parameter) that \code{\link[=pk.nca]{pk.nca()}} reads.
}
\details{
The reference interval gains whatever the linked calculation reads
from it (the plasma AUC for a renal clearance, for example) without being
announced, as calculating any dependency is.

An interval that already names a different reference for \code{param} is left
alone with a \code{pknca_warning_secondary_ref_exists} warning, so that the
helper never silently re-points an analysis.

With \code{reference = NULL} the reference is derived from the data, which needs
a \code{PKNCAdata} object (a bare intervals data.frame carries no concentrations
to derive it from).  The \code{group_ref} given to \code{\link[=PKNCAdata]{PKNCAdata()}} is used when it
is set.  Otherwise the derivation applies where the parameter is measured on
an interval collection while everything it references is a spot sample
(renal clearance) and the concentration data declare a collection \code{volume}:
each interval takes the profile with no collection volume that differs from
its own in the fewest group columns.  This is the same derivation
\code{\link[=pk.nca]{pk.nca()}} makes on its own, written out into the returned intervals
instead of being ephemeral.  Anything the derivation cannot resolve is an
error here (\code{pknca_error_secondary_needs_ref}) rather than the \code{NA} result
the automatic path gives, so narrow the request with \code{target_groups} when
some intervals are not meant to calculate \code{param}.

When the parameter pairs an interval collection with spot-sample
references (renal clearance:  an amount excreted against a plasma AUC)
and the reference interval is created by the \code{PKNCAdata} method, the
created interval spans the collections whole:  a collection that begins
inside the interval contributes its full amount (see \code{\link[=pk.nca]{pk.nca()}}), so a
collection running past the interval's \code{end} extends the created
reference's \code{end} to \code{time + duration} of the latest collection.  An
explicit \code{end} in \code{reference} overrides the extension, and the data.frame
method (which has no concentration data) copies the test interval's times
unchanged.
}
\section{Group columns that may differ}{


A reference is matched on the requesting group's own values in every
group column except the ones the reference interval names:  with groups
\code{treatment+subject} and \code{reference = data.frame(treatment = "iv")}, each
subject's oral profile uses that subject's IV profile.  When the
concentration data are also grouped by a column that the reference
treatment does not share, such as the period of a crossover, the IV
profile is in a different period than the oral one and is not found
(\code{pknca_error_secondary_ref_value_missing}).

\code{ref_vary} names such columns.  The reference is then matched on every
other group column and on the reference interval's own columns, and takes
whichever value of the varying columns has the reference interval's
results.  Exactly one may:  more than one (a replicate design giving the
reference twice) is an error (\code{pknca_error_secondary_ref_vary_ambiguous})
naming the requesting group and the candidates, which is resolved by
adding a group column that tells them apart to the intervals and naming
it in \code{reference}.  A varying column that the reference interval names
is pinned by it.  The subject column cannot vary, as a reference is always
the subject's own profile.

The declaration is stored per link as column names separated by commas
in the \verb{<param>_ref_vary} column of the intervals, so it can also be
written by hand.  \code{NA} there takes the \code{ref_vary} of \code{\link[=PKNCAdata]{PKNCAdata()}} (which
declares the varying columns once for every link of the data), and \code{""}
declares that no column varies.  The result names the reference's group
in its \verb{<group column>_ref} columns and in \code{PPANMETH}
(\code{"Reference interval: treatment=iv, period=1, 0-Inf"}).
}

